{"name":"Erosion","hex":"#ddd1bf","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"erosion","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The result of having been worn away or eroded, as by a glacier on rock or the sea on a cliff face.","The changing of a surface by mechanical action, friction, thermal expansion contraction, or impact."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/erosion"}],"entries":[{"title":"Erosion","blocks":[{"text":"Erosion is the action of surface processes that removes soil, rock, or dissolved material from one location on the Earth's crust and then transports it to another location where it is deposited. Erosion is distinct from weathering which involves no movement. Removal of rock or soil as clastic sediment is referred to as physical or mechanical erosion; this contrasts with chemical erosion, where soil or rock material is removed from an area by dissolution. Eroded sediment or solutes may be transported just a few millimetres, or for thousands of kilometres."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Eroding_rill_in_field_in_eastern_Germany.jpg/330px-Eroding_rill_in_field_in_eastern_Germany.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erosion"},{"title":"Erosion (morphology)","blocks":[{"text":"Erosion is one of two fundamental operations in morphological image processing from which all other morphological operations are based. It was originally defined for binary images, later being extended to grayscale images, and subsequently to complete lattices. The erosion operation usually uses a structuring element for probing and reducing the shapes contained in the input image."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Erosion.png/330px-Erosion.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erosion_(morphology)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/KharazaArch.jpg/500px-KharazaArch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/KharazaArch.jpg/1280px-KharazaArch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/KharazaArch.jpg/500px-KharazaArch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A natural arch produced by the wind erosion of differentially weathered rock in Jebel Kharaz, Jordan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKharazaArch.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Water_and_soil_splashed_by_the_impact_of_a_single_raindrop.jpg/500px-Water_and_soil_splashed_by_the_impact_of_a_single_raindrop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Water_and_soil_splashed_by_the_impact_of_a_single_raindrop.jpg/500px-Water_and_soil_splashed_by_the_impact_of_a_single_raindrop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Soil and water being splashed by the impact of a single raindrop","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWater_and_soil_splashed_by_the_impact_of_a_single_raindrop.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Rummu_aherainem%C3%A4gi2.jpg/500px-Rummu_aherainem%C3%A4gi2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Rummu_aherainem%C3%A4gi2.jpg/1280px-Rummu_aherainem%C3%A4gi2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Rummu_aherainem%C3%A4gi2.jpg/500px-Rummu_aherainem%C3%A4gi2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A spoil tip covered in rills and gullies due to erosion processes caused by rainfall: Rummu, Estonia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARummu_aherainem%C3%A4gi2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Dobbingstone_Burn_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1291882.jpg/500px-Dobbingstone_Burn_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1291882.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Dobbingstone_Burn_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1291882.jpg/500px-Dobbingstone_Burn_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1291882.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dobbingstone Burn, Scotland, showing two different types of erosion affecting the same place. Valley erosion is occurring due to the flow of the stream, and the boulders and stones (and much of the soil) that are lying on the stream's banks are glacial till that was left behind as ice age glaciers flowed over the terrain.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADobbingstone_Burn_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1291882.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Tauglbach_4.JPG/500px-Tauglbach_4.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Tauglbach_4.JPG/1280px-Tauglbach_4.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Tauglbach_4.JPG/500px-Tauglbach_4.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Layers of chalk exposed by a river eroding through them","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATauglbach_4.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Wavecut_platform_southerndown_pano.jpg/500px-Wavecut_platform_southerndown_pano.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Wavecut_platform_southerndown_pano.jpg/1280px-Wavecut_platform_southerndown_pano.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Wavecut_platform_southerndown_pano.jpg/500px-Wavecut_platform_southerndown_pano.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wave cut platform caused by erosion of cliffs by the sea, at Southerndown in South Wales","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWavecut_platform_southerndown_pano.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Pirunpes%C3%A4_Jalasj%C3%A4rvi_8.JPG/500px-Pirunpes%C3%A4_Jalasj%C3%A4rvi_8.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Pirunpes%C3%A4_Jalasj%C3%A4rvi_8.JPG/1280px-Pirunpes%C3%A4_Jalasj%C3%A4rvi_8.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Pirunpes%C3%A4_Jalasj%C3%A4rvi_8.JPG/500px-Pirunpes%C3%A4_Jalasj%C3%A4rvi_8.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Devil's Nest (Pirunpesä), the deepest ground erosion in Europe,[34] located in Jalasjärvi, Kurikka, Finland","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APirunpes%C3%A4_Jalasj%C3%A4rvi_8.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Grosser_Aletschgletscher_3196.JPG/500px-Grosser_Aletschgletscher_3196.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Grosser_Aletschgletscher_3196.JPG/1280px-Grosser_Aletschgletscher_3196.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Grosser_Aletschgletscher_3196.JPG/500px-Grosser_Aletschgletscher_3196.